{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210730,"nonce":"mHstONT/Ep4MLbSrJJZADxV322s=","title":"continue inside nested if inside for-in miscompiled","message":"Fixed in commit 1441d38. Record migrated from session memory 2026-09-12.\n\n\nWorkarounds in `@hatch:fp::dropWhile` and the\n`while (i \u003c n)` event drain in `@hatch:game` can revert to the\nnatural shape. Original investigation preserved below.\n\n---\n\nA `continue` statement placed inside a nested `if` that itself sits inside\na `for`-in loop body produces miscompiled bytecode / MIR. Manifests as\nruntime errors like **\"Bool does not implement 'call(_)'\"** or **\"List\ndoes not implement 'call(_)'\"** at apparently-unrelated lines within the\nloop body (assignments, even).\n\n**Minimal pattern that breaks:**\n\n```wren\nfor (x in seq) {\n  if (some_local_bool) {\n    if (fn.call(x)) continue\n    some_local_bool = false\n  }\n  out.add(x)\n}\n```\n\n**Workaround:** flatten the loop — don't use `continue` inside a nested\n`if` block. Rewrite with a separate while-loop that consumes the prefix\nand another that consumes the rest. Example in\n`@hatch:fp/fp.wren::dropWhile`.\n\n**Why it matters:** `continue` inside a single (non-nested) `if` inside\n`for` works fine (`@hatch:fp/fp.wren::distinctBy` uses that shape\nsafely). Only the NESTED-if path misbehaves.\n\n**How to apply:** if a future spec crashes with\n\"`SomeClass` does not implement 'call(_)'\" at a line that's clearly not\na call, check whether that loop body has `continue` inside a nested `if`.\nFlatten the control flow before chasing deeper.\n\n**Next step (someone else's turn):** build a minimal Wren repro outside\nof @hatch:fp, then track down whether it's MIR-build or bytecode-encode\nand fix in-tree.","files":null}]}